<h2>Your Photo Inspection Report</h2>
<p>After every inspection, you receive photos of what we found, with a plain-language explanation of each issue and how urgent it is. If something needs attention, the report comes with a written repair quote; if your roof is near the end of its life, we include an honest comparison of repair versus <a href="/services/roof-replacement/">roof replacement</a>. If your roof is in good shape, we tell you that too — and the photos become a useful baseline to compare against after the next storm.</p>

<div class="callout callout--tip"><p><strong>Keep your report.</strong> Dated photos of your roof in good condition can be valuable later. If hail hits next spring, "before" photos make it much easier to show your insurer what changed.</p></div>

<h2>Storm Damage and Insurance Inspections</h2>
<p>Spring is hail season in Central Texas, and storms often hit one neighborhood hard while sparing the next. After a storm, we look for the specific signs insurers and adjusters look for: circular bruises with granule loss, cracked shingle mats, dented vents, gutters and metal, and wind-creased tabs. Each one is photographed and noted by slope.</p>
<p>If the damage is significant, you can share our report with your insurance company, and we can meet the adjuster at your property to point out what we documented. We are roofers, not public adjusters — we do not negotiate your claim — but clear documentation helps make sure nothing gets missed. See our <a href="/services/roof-insurance-claims/">roof insurance claim help</a> page and our guide to <a href="/blog/insurance-coverage-for-storm-damaged-roofs/">insurance coverage for storm-damaged roofs</a>. Not sure whether last night’s storm was big enough to matter? Try the hail damage checker below.</p>

<h2>Real Estate Roof Inspections</h2>
<p>General home inspectors cover hundreds of items in a few hours, and many inspect the roof from a ladder or with a drone. That is a helpful start, but it is not the same as a roofer walking every slope. Buyers use our Austin roof inspection to understand how much life the roof really has left and whether repairs belong in negotiations. Sellers use it to fix small issues before listing so the roof does not become a sticking point. Either way, the report gives you specifics — not a vague "roof shows signs of wear."</p>

<h2>What We Most Often Find on Austin Roofs</h2>
<p>After years of inspecting roofs across Travis, Williamson and Hays counties, the same handful of issues show up again and again. None of them are visible from the street, and most are inexpensive to fix when caught early:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Cracked pipe boots.</strong> The rubber collars around plumbing vents dry out and split in the Texas sun, often years before the shingles wear out.</li>
  <li><strong>Unsealed or lifted shingles.</strong> Wind breaks the seal strips on edges and ridges, leaving tabs that flap and eventually blow off.</li>
  <li><strong>Debris-packed valleys.</strong> Oak leaves and cedar litter dam up water in valleys and behind chimneys.</li>
  <li><strong>Overheated attics.</strong> Blocked soffit vents or too little exhaust cook shingles from underneath.</li>
  <li><strong>Old repairs that failed.</strong> Roofing cement smeared over flashing gaps works for a season, then cracks.</li>
</ul>
<p>For landlords and property managers with several homes or a <a href="/services/multifamily-roofing/">multi-family property</a>, regular documented inspections turn these into planned maintenance items instead of tenant leak calls.</p>

<h2>How the Inspection Works</h2>
<ol class="steps">
  <li><h3>Book a time</h3><p>Call or request an inspection online. After big storms we prioritize active leaks and visible damage.</p></li>
  <li><h3>On-site inspection</h3><p>We walk the roof where it is safe to do so, check the attic if accessible, and photograph everything. Most homes take under an hour.</p></li>
  <li><h3>Review together</h3><p>We go over the photos with you on site or send them afterward, and answer every question.</p></li>
  <li><h3>Your decision</h3><p>Repair, monitor, or plan for replacement — there is no obligation and no pressure.</p></li>
</ol>

<h2>What You Can Safely Check Yourself</h2>
<p>Please stay off the roof — falls are a serious risk, especially on steep or hot surfaces. From the ground and inside the house, you can watch for:</p>
<ul class="checklist">
  <li>Shingle pieces, granules or ridge caps in the yard or gutters after a storm</li>
  <li>Dents in gutters, downspouts, AC units or a mailbox — signs hail was big enough to matter</li>
  <li>Ceiling stains, bubbling paint or a musty smell in upstairs rooms</li>
  <li>Daylight or damp insulation in the attic</li>
  <li>Sagging lines along the ridge or eaves</li>
</ul>
<p>Spotted any of these? Take our <a href="/roofing-tools/repair-or-replace-quiz/">Repair or Replace quiz</a>, then <a href="/contact-us/">schedule your free roof inspection</a> or call <a href="tel:+19564656045">(956) 465-6045</a>. We inspect roofs throughout Austin, <a href="/service-areas/round-rock-tx/">Round Rock</a>, Pflugerville, Cedar Park, Georgetown, Kyle and beyond.</p>
